{"authored" [{"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/review.json" "file_sha256" "e098f158118589010730b7eeed5b9954bc30a57a35e268620470e43d29cab7c3" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "612513e824effd8cfd7a57f4af30874dfa99ae7164748e2243a994a8c0b14d47" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/verification.json" "basis" 1737 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/review.json" "file_sha256" "e098f158118589010730b7eeed5b9954bc30a57a35e268620470e43d29cab7c3" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "612513e824effd8cfd7a57f4af30874dfa99ae7164748e2243a994a8c0b14d47" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10897/verification.json" "basis" 1737 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/review.json" "file_sha256" "8cdf63e1ccc2f9962560e55ad3076c5b25c4edc4c53d383dd48f0c8fb47e7e37" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "4d4f691a625fb2b1944610fd0e6bf4652987c084f8d43886c04cd0413a16aeab" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/verification.json" "basis" 1738 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/review.json" "file_sha256" "8cdf63e1ccc2f9962560e55ad3076c5b25c4edc4c53d383dd48f0c8fb47e7e37" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "4d4f691a625fb2b1944610fd0e6bf4652987c084f8d43886c04cd0413a16aeab" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10898/verification.json" "basis" 1738 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/review.json" "file_sha256" "408c688fdeb7565fcbca5bc600f357358c7a1c6be179094cc1554b7f5d361059" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "881f5ffd2b4749dab93362d969c575b17002c692170702458d6ae9226b1b4552" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/verification.json" "basis" 1739 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/review.json" "file_sha256" "408c688fdeb7565fcbca5bc600f357358c7a1c6be179094cc1554b7f5d361059" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "881f5ffd2b4749dab93362d969c575b17002c692170702458d6ae9226b1b4552" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-10911/verification.json" "basis" 1739 "rationale" "The actual example integrands are continuous, but the universal symmetry rule must require existence of the ordinary integral. Qualify the rule and explicitly distinguish ordinary improper convergence from principal-value regularization. Original source prompt, answer fields, and historical original solution remain preserved."} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals has Cauchy principal value zero due to symmetry? For every internal pole, remove an interval of equal radius on its two sides and take the radius to zero. This principal-value convention does not assert convergence of the ordinary improper integral.\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/review.json" "file_sha256" "283cb14e17d44e25b17cfb2e0f60fc523ac60570f898e51e39af03f8508a43b8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "2c40dc0420b178816f2a5098be8edb544e00e5f9a7a586656e187495a4401eed" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/verification.json" "basis" 1731 "rationale" "The source explicitly accepts II only after an actual Incorrect planned-W submission, but its ordinary improper tangent integrals have internal poles. The study prompt now states the symmetric Cauchy principal-value convention, and its worked solution distinguishes principal values from divergent ordinary improper integrals. Source key remains II only under corrected semantics, with new field/answer identities and preserved original versions."} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an integrable odd function, the ordinary integral over opposite limits is zero. When an interval contains poles, odd symmetry alone does not prove that the ordinary improper integral exists. This question instead specifies Cauchy principal values, with equal exclusions on both sides of each internal pole.\n\nI. The continuous function $\\sin^2 x$ is nonnegative and not identically zero on $[-2,2]$. Its integral is $2-\\frac12\\sin4>0$, so I is not zero.\n\nII. The function $\\tan(2x)$ is odd. Its poles in $(-3,3)$ are $-3\\pi/4,-\\pi/4,\\pi/4,3\\pi/4$. The remaining domain is symmetric when equal intervals are removed about these paired poles, so its integral is zero before taking the limit. Hence the specified principal value is zero. Its ordinary improper integral diverges because the one-sided integrals at each pole do not converge separately.\n\nIII. The function $4\\tan x$ is odd, but $[-2,4]$ is not symmetric. Its internal poles are $-\\pi/2$ and $\\pi/2$. Using the antiderivative $-4\\ln|\\cos x|$ on the continuous pieces, equal two-sided exclusions at each pole cancel their divergent terms. The principal value is\n\n$$4\\ln\\left|\\frac{\\cos2}{\\cos4}\\right|,$$\n\nwhich lies between $-1.81$ and $-1.80$ and is not zero. The ordinary improper integral again diverges.\n\nTherefore, under the stated principal-value convention, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/review.json" "file_sha256" "283cb14e17d44e25b17cfb2e0f60fc523ac60570f898e51e39af03f8508a43b8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "2c40dc0420b178816f2a5098be8edb544e00e5f9a7a586656e187495a4401eed" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/verification.json" "basis" 1731 "rationale" "The source explicitly accepts II only after an actual Incorrect planned-W submission, but its ordinary improper tangent integrals have internal poles. The study prompt now states the symmetric Cauchy principal-value convention, and its worked solution distinguishes principal values from divergent ordinary improper integrals. Source key remains II only under corrected semantics, with new field/answer identities and preserved original versions."} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/review.json" "file_sha256" "283cb14e17d44e25b17cfb2e0f60fc523ac60570f898e51e39af03f8508a43b8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "2c40dc0420b178816f2a5098be8edb544e00e5f9a7a586656e187495a4401eed" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-88686/verification.json" "basis" 1731 "rationale" "The source explicitly accepts II only after an actual Incorrect planned-W submission, but its ordinary improper tangent integrals have internal poles. The study prompt now states the symmetric Cauchy principal-value convention, and its worked solution distinguishes principal values from divergent ordinary improper integrals. Source key remains II only under corrected semantics, with new field/answer identities and preserved original versions."} {"question" "q-88698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/state.json" "file_sha256" "50459f288de63b68678b5cd50d67e9bef576e2c3c7d1404b817e2765ab1c3bad" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/re