0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\,{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =-2(-x)^{4} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{10}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{2}}{1+(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =((-x)+1)^{3} \\\\  & =(1-x)^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-88695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "c