3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-334370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-A"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}A"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "A"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|A|"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-A"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({2}^{(-x)}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{(-x)}}) \\\\  & =({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{(1/2)^{x}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}-{2}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{3}}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-334354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-334354" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "695c9a2f231761c8f243abfac54df5a4553eb3b4e729f309ec9a167d9fef3aec"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb33222b7d9f2af2a0fa7d1341aa77d218d68a9b4d5ea34b91bf6ebc6f5cc27b"} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "6beadedc3eb21bb8103166bfb9581b2661cf74be0d41bd96f05b1e1cb2292b22"} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "6beadedc3eb21bb8103166bfb9581b2661cf74be0d41bd96f05b1e1cb2292b22"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "2feb58b40d68ff5d01748c88505125479db242d08fa94b59ef74d5e44af4a6a2"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "2feb58b40d68ff5d01748c88505125479db242d08fa94b59ef74d5e44af4a6a2"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "73fcabcce9aacdabe9ced2be0d72be15d864d6a4a590e31ec85707f7dcfcacfa"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "73fcabcce9aacdabe9ced2be0d72be15d864d6a4